      "summary": "A5 is rescoped to the two production iOS rows and hands the probe pair to A18, which it had been contradicting since r5. A9 is split into A9a (the four macOS Mac Development profiles, device list compared against the rows), A9b (the four iOS development profiles, deferred under ADR-024 and not created at C1) and A9c (the two Developer ID profiles, ProvisionsAllDevices). Neither fix is left as a corrected string: rule S2 gives every scope-bearing assertion a structured scope derived three ways from the authoritative rows, R35 and R36 recompute and compare them, and the partition check that A5 and A18 were violating is now run for every registered entitlement key. No entitlement decision, identifier, record, profile row or C1 authorization changed.",
      "verifiedIndependently": "Both findings were re-derived from the rows before being accepted rather than taken on trust. F1: the application-groups status of ios.probe.host and ios.probe.provider is 'prohibited', so A5's 'every iOS bundle' and A18's 'ABSENT from both iOS probe bundles' cannot both hold. CONFIRMED. F2: all four iOS profile rows declare development.devices as ['deferred — ADR-024'] while the four macOS rows declare ['this Apple-silicon Mac'], so A9's universal claim is false for half the rows. CONFIRMED. Registering A6's scope then exposed a third instance of the same class that no verdict had reported — the rationale document's A6 row still granting macos.host the keychain group r5 removed — which is recorded in S2.staleClaimsFound and fixed here.",
