      "verdict": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_reviewer-verdict-05.md",
      "summary": "Two contract-consistency corrections. No entitlement decision changed and no target row moved. F1: crossPlatformRules[2] still asserted the superseded r4 Keychain contract - the shared access group granted to the macOS host - while the target rows, rule K2, the record's consumedByTargets, assertions A6/A17 and the r5 revision log all said iOS-only. The sentence is corrected, and the DEFECT CLASS is closed rather than the string patched: new object crossPlatformSharingContract (rule S1) gives every cross-platform sentence a structured rule, and new gate R32 derives each grant set from the target rows, cross-checks it against the record's own consumer list, then requires the prose grantClause verbatim, requires the display names inside it to equal the derived set, and requires every other target named anywhere in the sentence to be a DECLARED non-grantee mention carrying its own clause. R33 requires the same grant clauses to appear in the human-readable rationale document, so the two artifacts cannot drift either. The second half of the class - a stale record COUNT - is closed by a scan for App Group count claims, each of which must sit at a registered path and agree with the record list; appGroupStyleRule's 'one record per family' was the surviving instance and is rewritten. F2: TASK-260715-3jloqy, the unattended task that spends the C1 authorization, still carried the pre-r5 capability contract - create shared groups, provision App Group and Keychain capabilities, AC1 requiring shared groups to exist, and AC2 requiring the packet-tunnel entitlement only on provider identifiers. Its description, scope, acceptance criteria and checklist are corrected on the board to c1AuthorizationScope exactly, and the requirement is pinned here as portalMutationTaskContract (rule P1) with a durable gate script, so the check verdict 05 ran by hand is reproducible.",
      "verifiedIndependently": "Both findings were re-checked against the r5 artifact before being accepted, not taken on trust. F1: the reviewer's jq gate was re-run against r5 and returned false, exit 1; a sweep of EVERY string in the r5 JSON for stale-r4 grant or count claims then found exactly two survivors - crossPlatformRules[2] and appGroupStyleRule.statement's 'per family' - which is why the fix is a rule over all six sentences plus a count scan rather than two string edits. CONFIRMED. F2: TASK-260715-3jloqy's board record was read in full and all four quoted statements are present verbatim; its dependency edges confirm it is blocked by this matrix and by TASK-260728-q5kjta, so it executes after Ceremony C1 with exactly this contract in hand. The AC2 half was checked independently of the r5 changes: this contract's own target rows require com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension on all eight targets, hosts included, and R7 has enforced that since r2 - so 'only on provider identifiers' contradicted r1 as much as it contradicts r6, and would have under-provisioned both macOS host App IDs. CONFIRMED.",
