      "summary": "com.apple.security.application-groups is now PROHIBITED on all four macOS targets and required on iOS only (new rule G4). The macOS credential transport question is closed: TASK-260728-7ii1xz selected the file-based system-domain keychain seeded over the NE app-message channel, with the non-secret snapshot travelling in providerConfiguration, and rejected the XPC candidate. None of the six grants the App Group entitlement actually confers on macOS is used by that design, so the capability has no current function on any macOS target and is withheld. Ceremony C1 no longer creates any App Group record or enables the App Groups capability on any macOS App ID. keychain-access-groups on macos.provider moves from prohibited-pending-decision to a settled prohibited, and loses the resolutionOwner and amendmentRule that existed only to keep it open; rule K1 replaces that amendmentRule with the correct, narrow reopening condition. OC-1 is closed; OC-4 is downgraded. Every accepted r2/r3 decision - the four production identifiers and their containment, channel-keyed Network Extension values, the registered iOS-style App Group literals, the legacy no-migration rule, the Sparkle Mach lookup exception and rule X1, profile classes, and C1's iOS exclusions - is preserved.",
      "verifiedIndependently": "The finding was re-checked before being accepted, not taken on trust. The macOS App Group grant set was read directly out of /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71): lines 302-330 and 459-465 are the only two places the entitlement appears, and they confer exactly six grants (recorded field-by-field in appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.survey). Three are home-relative and therefore break across the root/user boundary; three are system-wide and are used by no candidate the transport decision kept. The claim that a root provider needs no keychain entitlement was likewise re-verified locally: application.sb:792-793 grants /Library/Keychains read+write to any com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension holder, and application.sb:655-656 grant the securityd/SecurityServer Mach lookups to every sandboxed process, exactly as TASK-260728-7ii1xz reported. CONFIRMED.",
