    "conditionalExceptionPolicy": "A conditional entry is REVIEWED BUT NOT GRANTED. It is disjoint from reviewedExceptions by construction: reviewedExceptions is the ACTIVE register and must equal the set of temporary exceptions the target rows actually author, which is what rule R26 enforces and what keeps the active register honest. A conditional entry authors nothing, appears in no allowlist, moves no count, and must be ABSENT from every signed bundle while armed is false. Its purpose is narrow and is the point of the whole construction: the spec bans an UNREVIEWED exception entitlement, so an exception that a supported OS turns out to require must already have been reviewed, or the correct requirement gets rejected as drift the day the evidence arrives. Arming moves the entry into reviewedExceptions and the row into required-adjacent in one amendment under section 11; it never happens as a local build setting, and it is not a fresh review round because the review is this entry.",
    "conditionalExceptionDerivation": {
      "statement": "Every field of a conditional exception entry is DERIVED from elsewhere in this contract or bounded semantically. None of them is established by comparing the register entry to its own copy on the target row.",
      "why": "Reviewer verdict 09 F1. r10 required the register and the row to agree and stopped there, so a COORDINATED edit of both copies passed the gate: the path widened from /Library/Keychains/ to /, the developer-id channel disappeared, and the arming owner moved to an unrelated task - and validate-matrix.py exited 0 on all three, where a negative control must exit 1. Equality between two copies of a value is a consistency check, not a review. What makes X1-C1 PRE-AUTHORIZED is that this contract reviewed one exact exception; a field that can move without failing a gate is not the field that was reviewed, so the pre-authorization would launder whatever the field became. Verdict 10 F1 then found the one field this contract had documented as unclosed rather than closed. reviewedIn was bounded to membership in the issued revision set, and the paragraph below it said so under a Stated bound heading - the gate cannot tell WHICH issued revision reviewed an entry. The reviewer moved it from r10 to r2, a revision that predates the conditional register entirely, and the validator exited 0. Writing a gap down is not closing it: the derivation reads as complete and the field it left open is the one that makes the entry pre-authorized at all.",
      "principle": "A derivation is only worth having if it bottoms out on data a coordinated edit cannot simply restate. Four kinds qualify and this register uses all four: a SEMANTIC bound, where the test is a property of the value rather than a match against another copy of it (a path may not be an ancestor of the reviewed path, so widening fails however many copies agree); a set owned by a DIFFERENT and independently gated part of this contract (the target's signing channels, held by rules NE1, R7, R19, R22, R23 and the profile rules); an EXTERNAL one (a declared consumer, which rule D1 requires to exist on the live board and to reach this task transitively); and a HISTORICAL one, added in r12 for verdict 10 - the digest-pinned per-revision baselines attached to this task, which are immutable inputs to the preservation harnesses, so the revision an entry entered in is computed from files no edit to this contract can reach. A field checked only against its own mirror is none of the four and is recorded here as such.",
      "appliesTo": "every entry in conditionalExceptions, not only X1-C1",
      "gate": "R39",
      "fields": [
        {
          "field": "target",
          "boundKind": "derived-unique-row",
          "derivedFrom": "the unique target row carrying an osVersionScope whose rule equals the entry's governedBy",
          "whyNotACopy": "The K3 version scope block is pinned to the macos.provider keychain-access-groups row by R39's settled-row checks, which fail closed if it is absent there. Moving the entry to another target therefore means first deleting the block those checks require, which fails first."
        },
        {
          "field": "key",
          "boundKind": "literal-and-cross-checked",
          "value": "com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write",
          "derivedFrom": "keychainSandboxFloorRule.ifGrantAbsentAtFloor, which must name this key verbatim",
          "whyThisKey": "The base grant read on the verified OS is file-read* AND file-write* on the keychain path. A read-only exception would not reach the transport TASK-260728-7ii1xz selected, and a different exception family is a different review altogether.",
          "whyNotACopy": "The key is pinned in the gate and cross-checked against rule K3's own arming instruction, so drifting it means drifting the rule that authorises it, in a sentence a reader sees."
        },
        {
          "field": "valuesIfArmed",
          "boundKind": "path-subtree",
          "reviewedPath": "/Library/Keychains/",
          "maxValues": 1,
          "bannedValues": [
            "/",
            "/Library",
            "/Library/"
          ],
          "rule": "every value must lie at or inside the reviewed path; no value may be an ancestor of it, and the filesystem root is banned outright",
          "derivedFrom": "the path keychainSandboxFloorRule.verifiedOn cites in the grant it actually read, which R39 independently requires that field to name",
          "whyNotACopy": "An ancestor test is a property of the value. Widening to / or /Library fails it however many copies agree - which is precisely what verdict 09's first mutation proved r10 could not do."
        },
        {
          "field": "channelsIfArmed",
          "boundKind": "equals-target-channels",
          "derivedFrom": "targets[<the derived target>].channels, in full",
          "whyFullSet": "The floor question is about the BASE OS App Sandbox profile. A base profile does not vary by signing channel, so if the grant is absent it is absent on every channel the target signs on. A subset would leave one channel broken on the same OS - and dropping developer-id specifically would break the shipping provider while the development lane looked fine.",
          "whyNotACopy": "The target's channel list is held by the Network Extension and profile rules. Removing developer-id there to launder this field breaks those instead, which is the point of deriving from a set somebody else owns."
        },
        {
          "field": "armedBy",
          "boundKind": "equals-rule-owner-and-declared-consumer",
          "derivedFrom": "the element id inside keychainSandboxFloorRule.resolutionOwner, which must also appear in consumers[]",
          "whyNotACopy": "Rule D1 requires every declared consumer to exist on the live board and to depend transitively on this task, so an invented or merely convenient arming owner fails on the board gate as well as here. Verdict 09's third mutation moved the owner to TASK-260715-9yp8to - a real consumer, which is why a shape check on the element id could not see it."
        },
        {
          "field": "governedBy",
          "boundKind": "literal",
          "value": "K3",
          "derivedFrom": "the rule that states the OS-version scope this entry answers",
          "whyNotACopy": "Pinned in the gate; an entry governed by nothing is unreviewable."
