    "exceptionReviewProvenance": {
      "id": "X1-P",
      "statement": "reviewedIn is not a label a revision writes about itself. For every entry in EITHER of rule X1's registers it is DERIVED: it must equal the revision in which that entry first entered this contract, and that revision is established from evidence that is older than the entry's claim about it.",
      "why": "Reviewer verdict 10 F1. r11 required reviewedIn to equal ANY revision this contract had issued. That is weaker than r11's own derivation contract said the field needed: conditionalExceptionDerivation already recorded that pointing reviewedIn at an older revision can imply a review that did not happen, and then gated it at membership anyway. r11 wrote the gap down under a Stated bound heading instead of closing it. The reviewer moved X1-C1.reviewedIn from 2026-07-28.r10 to 2026-07-28.r2 - a revision that predates the conditional register entirely - and the validator reported PASS and exited 0, where a negative control must exit 1. An entry whose review revision can drift is an entry whose pre-authorization launders whatever revision it comes to name, which breaks the evidence chain behind arming.",
      "principle": "An introduction is a historical fact about this contract, so the evidence for it must be older than the revision that claims it. Two evidence classes qualify and this register uses both. SNAPSHOT-PROVEN is the strong one: the per-revision baselines attached to this task are immutable inputs to the preservation harnesses, so the revision an entry first appears in is COMPUTED - absent from one snapshot, present in the next - and no edit to the live contract can reach it. SUMMARY-ATTESTED is the weaker fallback, available only for an entry older than the oldest attached snapshot: the oldest revisionLog entry whose append-only summary names the exception key verbatim. Both are then cross-checked against a declared introduction record on the revision log, so the label, the evidence and the record are three places a coordinated edit has to reach and a fourth gate compares.",
      "boundStated": "What this does NOT establish, stated rather than implied. It does not prove the review recorded in that revision was a GOOD review; it proves the entry names the revision it actually entered in. Summary-attested is genuinely weaker than snapshot-proven - it rests on append-only prose rather than on a digest-pinned file - which is why the evidence class is declared per entry and why the gate REFUSES summary-attested for any entry the snapshot chain can decide. And the chain has a horizon: it begins at the oldest attached baseline, so an entry older than that is decidable only by attestation until an older snapshot is attached.",
      "snapshotChain": [
        {
          "revision": "2026-07-28.r8",
          "resource": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_r8-baseline.json",
          "sha256": "68e9aeeba174ab6bd094d6cb616db7a72c19270a4df72c1f1a3c6cbeb723995f"
        },
        {
          "revision": "2026-07-28.r9",
          "resource": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_r9-baseline.json",
          "sha256": "1c290fcd7c07fc276ece1110a84f6cb565ba0534934b6cad02e95c92257e8c9c"
        },
        {
          "revision": "2026-07-28.r10",
          "resource": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_r10-baseline.json",
          "sha256": "cb7e7b7278d24f97292a00dd901ab1c9e4a4eb78832e67fb142acedda9e30324"
        },
        {
          "revision": "2026-07-28.r11",
          "resource": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_r11-baseline.json",
          "sha256": "66693cd855c45c9125116aef4eeb25b4d9831a03c66153f391d4b5aabb34bd23"
        }
      ],
      "digestForm": "sha256 over json.dumps(parsed, sort_keys=True, separators=(',', ':'), ensure_ascii=False), so the pin is over CONTENT and survives reformatting while still failing on any change to a value.",
      "snapshotChainRule": "The chain is ordered oldest-first, holds no repeated revision, and its newest element is exactly this contract's supersedes - so a bump that does not attach the superseded revision as a baseline fails closed rather than quietly shortening the evidence. Every declared snapshot must exist beside this contract and match its digest; a missing or altered snapshot is a FAILURE, never a skip, because a chain that cannot be read proves nothing. Presence of an entry in a snapshot is tested by (register, key, target), which every revision of both registers has carried, rather than by the entry id, which is new in r12 for the active register. The chain may not be NARROWED either, which is the other way to launder an introduction: it covers a contiguous run of revisions, so a gap cannot be opened in the middle to make an entry undecidable and demote it to attestation, and every baseline sitting beside this contract must be declared in it, so evidence cannot be left undeclared on disk. Narrowing the chain is an amendment under section 11, visible in a diff, never a quiet deletion.",
      "monotonicityRule": "An entry's presence across the chain must be monotone: once present it is present in every later snapshot and in this contract. A register entry that disappeared and came back has two introductions and no single revision reviewed it, so the derivation refuses to pick one.",
      "introductionRecord": "Every revisionLog entry declares `introduces`, the register entry ids that first appeared in that revision. Exactly one revision may introduce a given id, every id in either register must be introduced by exactly one revision, and no revision may claim an id that is in no register. The record is a CROSS-CHECK on the derivation, not the derivation itself - it is inside the file it describes, so alone it would be exactly the self-attestation verdict 10 rejected.",
      "entries": [
        {
          "entry": "X1-A1",
          "register": "reviewedExceptions",
          "key": "com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name",
          "target": "macos.host",
          "introducedIn": "2026-07-28.r3",
          "evidenceClass": "summary-attested",
          "attestingLiteral": "com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name",
          "why": "The entry predates the oldest attached snapshot: it is present in r8, r9, r10 and r11 alike, so the chain cannot decide it. The r3 revisionLog entry is the oldest whose summary names the key verbatim, and r2's does not name it at all."
        },
        {
          "entry": "X1-C1",
          "register": "conditionalExceptions",
          "key": "com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write",
          "target": "macos.provider",
          "introducedIn": "2026-07-28.r10",
          "evidenceClass": "snapshot-proven",
          "why": "Absent from the r8 and r9 snapshots, present in r10 and in every snapshot since. This is the entry verdict 10 named: its introducing revision is now computed from files outside the contract, so moving reviewedIn to r2 fails on evidence no edit to this JSON can reach."
        }
      ],
      "evidenceClasses": [
        "snapshot-proven",
        "summary-attested"
      ],
      "gates": {
        "reviewedExceptions": "R26",
        "conditionalExceptions": "R39"
      },
      "gateAssignmentNote": "Each register is gated by the rule that already owns it, so a drifted reviewedIn is reported under the rule a reader would look up - R26 for the active register, R39 for the conditional one. The structural checks on this rule itself are reported under R39, which is where verdict 10 required the conditional control to land.",
      "source": "Reviewer verdict 10 F1, required rework items 1-3.",
      "residualBound": "An edit coordinated across a baseline, its pinned digest, the introduction record and the entry would pass THIS validator. It does not pass the preservation harnesses, which consume the same baselines for a different reason: injecting the conditional entry into the r9 baseline makes preserve.py exit 1 on a changed exceptionEntitlementRule and preserve-r10.py exit 1 on `conditionalExceptions is not new in r10`. Both were run against a tampered baseline rather than assumed. The bound is recorded because a cross-harness catch is a weaker guarantee than a gate: it holds only while those harnesses are run and their baselines attached, which is why rule N1's harness counts and the snapshot chain's completeness check both exist."
    }
