  "environmentRules": {
    "identifierPerConfiguration": false,
    "forbiddenIdentifierSuffixes": [
      ".debug",
      ".dev",
      ".staging",
      ".beta",
      ".internal",
      ".release"
    ],
    "statement": "One bundle identifier per target across every build configuration. Debug and release are distinguished ONLY by signing channel — signing identity class, profile class, hardened-runtime state, get-task-allow, and the channel-keyed Network Extension entitlement value — never by identifier.",
    "rationale": [
      "macOS system-extension user approval is keyed to team identifier + extension bundle identifier; a per-configuration suffix multiplies physical approval friction on Hold H2 and H3.",
      "TASK-260715-9yp8to must exercise the same identity that ships.",
      "Fewer App IDs and Network Extension profiles to keep valid."
    ],
    "exception": "The disposable probe family is a distinct identity, development-signed only, never Developer ID signed, never notarized, never uploaded. It exists precisely so the production identity is not burned on throwaway approvals. A consequence of rule NE1 is that the probe can only ever prove the development-channel entitlement shape.",
    "versionRule": "Host and its embedded provider MUST carry identical CFBundleShortVersionString and CFBundleVersion; the monotonic build sequence is owned by ADR-018 / TASK-260715-1tzaed."
  },
  "crossPlatformRules": [
    "Bundle identifiers are NOT shared across platforms. There is no multiplatform/universal-purchase App ID; four production identifiers, one per target.",
    "ONE App Group record exists in this contract, group.works.relux.tunnel, and its iOS-style literal is identical on both platforms (rule G1) - but the ENTITLEMENT is granted to the iOS host and the iOS provider only (rule G4). On macOS the root provider and the user-context host resolve different containers and the selected transport uses no App Group mechanism, so all four macOS targets are prohibited. The iOS probe host and the iOS probe provider are prohibited too, because TASK-260715-1jckn0 names no shared container of its own (rule G4 probeRule). The record stays defined here so the iOS production row re-arms unchanged under ADR-024.",
    "One Keychain access group NAME, works.relux.tunnel.shared, granted to the iOS host and the iOS provider only, and to NO macOS target on either channel, in either family (rules K1 and K2). Never the macOS provider, which reads the system-domain keychain, where access groups do not apply (rule K1). Every macOS target uses the default access group its injected application identifier provides; see rule K2 for why that costs it nothing.",
    "The Network Extension entitlement VALUE is a function of the signing channel, not the platform (rule NE1). Every target on both platforms requires the entitlement; only the value differs. macOS development signing and both iOS channels use the unsuffixed value; only direct Developer ID signing uses the suffixed value.",
    "The Sparkle Mach lookup exception is NOT shared at all (rule X1). It is granted to the macOS host alone, because Sparkle is linked into that target alone; iOS updates through the App Store and no packet-tunnel target updates anything.",
    "ReluxTunnelCore MUST NOT hardcode any identifier. Values are injected through PlatformVPNIdentity (Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/VPNManagerRepository.swift:8); PlatformVPNIdentity.production(for:) stays throwing until TASK-260715-1tzaed binds these values."
  ],
  "crossPlatformSharingContract": {
    "id": "S1",
    "statement": "A cross-platform summary sentence carries NO independent authority. Every grant it states is DERIVED from the target rows and from the record lists, and rule R32 recomputes the derivation and compares it against the sentence before the contract is allowed to pass. A summary may not name a target as a grantee unless the target row grants it, and may not name a target at all unless the mention is registered below as a declared non-grantee contrast.",
    "history": "Reviewer verdict 05 F1. Through r5 the cross-platform summary was six free-text strings that nothing checked. r5 corrected eight target rows, two rules, two assertions and the C1 scope, and left crossPlatformRules[2] asserting the superseded r4 contract - that the shared Keychain access group is granted to the macOS host. The 846-check r5 validator passed, because no rule looked at the summary at all. The defect class is a prose summary that ASSERTS a grant set instead of RENDERING one; the fix is to make every such sentence a checked projection of the data rather than a second, unsynchronised statement of it.",
    "mechanism": "Each entry in crossPlatformRules has exactly one structured rule here, addressed by renderedIndex. The structured rule names the entitlement key it summarises; R32 derives the granted target set from the target rows, compares it with grantedTargets, compares that in turn with the record's own consumedByTargets, and then checks the prose: the grantClause must appear VERBATIM in the rendered sentence, the display names inside the grantClause must be exactly the granted targets, and every other target named anywhere in the sentence must appear in nonGranteeMentions with its own clause present verbatim. A stale sentence therefore fails on the clause, on the mention, or on both.",
    "displayNameDerivation": "A target's display name is derived from its own row - platform label ('iOS' or 'macOS'), then ' probe' for the probe family, then the role. macos.host is 'macOS host', ios.probe.provider is 'iOS probe provider'. R32 also treats the raw target key as a mention, so 'macos.host' and 'the macOS host' are both caught. No display name is a substring of another, so the scan cannot mis-attribute a mention.",
    "authorityNote": "grantClause is normative and machine-checked. The remaining prose in a rendered sentence is explanatory and carries no grant: it may not introduce a target that is not either a grantee or a declared non-grantee mention, which is the only way explanatory prose could smuggle a grant back in.",
    "recordCountClaimRule": "A sentence that states how many App Group records exist is a count claim. R32 scans every string in this contract for one, and every hit must sit at a path listed in recordCountClaimPaths with a number equal to the actual record count. That is what closes the second half of the class: r5 deleted the probe record and left two sentences still saying 'one record per family', which was true of r4's two-record world and false of r5's one-record world.",
    "historicalPaths": [
      "revisionLog",
      "appGroupDisjointnessRule.history",
      "crossPlatformSharingContract.history",
      "crossPlatformSharingContract.recordCountClaimRule"
    ],
    "historicalPathsNote": "These paths describe the state of an EARLIER revision on purpose and are pinned to it, so a count they mention is not a claim about the current data. Everything else is scanned. The exclusion list is declared here rather than hard-coded in the validator, so widening it is a visible edit to the contract.",
    "recordCountClaimPaths": [
      "crossPlatformRules[1]",
      "c1AuthorizationScope.appGroupNote"
    ],
    "source": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_reviewer-verdict-05.md finding F1, required rework items 1-4.",
