        "records": null,
        "recordSource": "keychainAccessGroups",
        "derivation": "This is the sentence verdict 05 F1 caught: through r5 it read 'the iOS host and appex, and the macOS host', which was the r4 contract. grantedTargets is derived from the target rows and must equal keychainAccessGroups[0].consumedByTargets, which R31 independently ties back to the rows.",
        "whyTheMacOSHostIsNotNamed": "Deliberate. The exclusion is stated universally - no macOS target - rather than target by target, because NAMING the macOS host in this sentence is exactly what produced the r5 defect: r4 named it as a grantee, r5 moved the row, and the name stayed. A universal clause has nothing to go stale, and R32 derives it from all eight rows rather than from a list someone has to remember to edit. The host-specific reasoning belongs to rule K2 and to D-8 in the rationale document, which is where a reader who wants it will look. The reviewer's own verdict-05 gate asserts the same thing textually - that this sentence no longer contains 'macOS host' - and it now passes."
