  "openConstraints": [
    {
      "id": "OC-1",
      "severity": "closed-was-architecture-blocking-for-macOS-credentials",
      "statement": "The macOS packet-tunnel system extension runs as root in a non-user context. The Data Protection Keychain is available only to code running in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the SAME user. The .spec/security-privacy.md design — private keys and passphrases in the Data Protection Keychain shared through one access group with the packet tunnel extension — therefore CANNOT work between the macOS host and the macOS provider. It works unchanged on iOS.",
      "corroboration": "Observed 2026-07-28 on this Mac. (a) Two shipping Developer ID packet-tunnel system extensions ship com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write entries for /private/var/root/Library/Group Containers/ (Tailscale, Surfshark) — direct evidence that a root provider's group container is not the user's. (b) Surfshark's provider carries a temporary-exception path for /Library/Keychains/, i.e. the FILE-BASED System keychain, not the Data Protection Keychain. (c) PureVPN's providers carry keychain-access-groups outright. Together these indicate the real-world macOS pattern is a file-based/System keychain or an IPC seed, not a shared Data Protection Keychain access group.",
      "affects": [
        "TASK-260715-1o9wjz",
        "TASK-260715-379cpk",
        "TASK-260715-29ws8l",
        "TASK-260715-2hhh7x",
        "TASK-260715-3f4lxy"
      ],
      "matrixEffect": "closed. macos.provider keychain-access-groups is prohibited and settled; no other row is affected.",
      "status": "closed",
      "resolvedBy": "TASK-260728-7ii1xz",
      "resolvedIn": "2026-07-28.r4",
      "resolution": "Confirmed as stated and then resolved: the .spec/security-privacy.md shared Data-Protection-Keychain-access-group design does not work between the macOS host and a root provider, and works unchanged on iOS. The replacement is candidate D - the provider owns a file-based system-domain keychain item, seeded once from the host over NETunnelProviderSession.sendProviderMessage. Matrix consequence: macos.provider keychain-access-groups is settled prohibited (rule K1), not pending. Downstream tasks 379cpk, 1o9wjz, 29ws8l, 2hhh7x and 3f4lxy each revise the assumption named in TASK-260728-7ii1xz_macos-credential-transport-decision.md section 8."
    },
    {
      "id": "OC-2",
      "severity": "verify-at-first-build",
      "statement": "A sandboxed containing app carrying com.apple.developer.system-extension.install is reported working; the common failure mode is an invalid (not expired) provisioning profile producing a spurious 'would like to access data from other apps' prompt. Treat an invalid profile as the first hypothesis, not a sandbox incompatibility.",
      "corroboration": "All three shipping Developer ID hosts inspected on this Mac are NOT sandboxed, so none exercises this combination. The sandboxed-host plus system-extension.install shape is this project's own, from .spec/platform-distribution.md, and has no local precedent to lean on.",
      "affects": [
        "TASK-260715-1r0fxv",
        "TASK-260715-9yp8to"
      ],
      "resolutionOwner": "TASK-260715-9yp8to",
      "matrixEffect": "none"
    },
    {
      "id": "OC-3",
      "severity": "verify-at-portal",
      "statement": "The team's App ID Prefix is assumed equal to the Team ID 262RZ595FP. Legacy teams can differ. TASK-260715-3jloqy must record the observed prefix and raise a blocker on mismatch rather than adapting silently.",
      "affects": [
        "TASK-260715-3jloqy"
      ],
      "resolutionOwner": "TASK-260715-3jloqy",
      "matrixEffect": "every $(AppIdentifierPrefix) resolvedLiteral"
    },
    {
      "id": "OC-4",
      "severity": "verify-only-if-XPC-is-ever-selected",
      "statement": "If a sandboxed XPC Mach service is EVER selected as the macOS host-to-provider channel, the endpoint name must be prefixed by an App Group ID the sandboxed process is entitled to. TASK-260728-7ii1xz evaluated that channel as candidate C and rejected it, and rule G4 now prohibits com.apple.security.application-groups on every macOS target, so no macOS target holds a group prefix today and the question is dormant rather than pending. Every Apple example of the pattern found, and all three shipping products inspected on this Mac, use a macOS-style Team-ID-prefixed group for the prefix - and all of those predate the Feb 2025 change.",
      "affects": [
        "TASK-260715-uyju7n",
        "TASK-260715-3f4lxy"
      ],
      "resolutionOwner": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo",
      "matrixEffect": "none - dormant. macOS App Groups are prohibited under rule G4, so no macOS target carries a group prefix. If XPC is ever revived, that is a G4 reopening case (a) and this matrix is amended under section 11; only then does the iOS-style-prefix question need settling, and if an iOS-style prefix were rejected the amendment would add a SEPARATE, unregistrable macOS-style identifier for that single purpose, leaving the registered iOS-style records untouched.",
      "newEvidenceR4": "Read at /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71): application.sb:326-328 builds the authorized prefix as (global-name-prefix (string-append suite \".\")) where suite is the literal entitlement value, whatever its style. On the SANDBOX side the mechanism is therefore style-agnostic, and an iOS-style group.works.relux.tunnel would yield the prefix 'group.works.relux.tunnel.'. This is evidence, not proof: the launchd bootstrap-registration side and the profile-authorization side were not exercised, and nothing was signed. Recorded so a future revival of the XPC channel starts from what is known rather than from the r3 statement that no source addressed it at all.",
      "notAStopTheLine": "The question is only reachable if a rejected candidate is revived, it has a named owner, and it does not block Ceremony C1 - C1 no longer grants the App Groups capability at all."
    },
    {
      "id": "OC-5",
      "severity": "verify-on-minimum-os-lane",
      "statement": "The macOS provider's freedom from any keychain-related entitlement depends on the base App Sandbox profile granting /Library/Keychains to Network Extension processes. That grant is read and confirmed on macOS 26.5 (25F71) and is unverified on the binding macOS 15.0 floor. If it is absent there, the provider needs the temporary file exception pre-authorized as register entry X1-C1.",
      "corroboration": "Not resolvable from installed products. Surfshark's provider ships the exception with a 10.15 minimum, PureVPN's two carry keychain-access-groups instead, and Tailscale's carries neither at a 12.4 minimum - support ranges far wider than this project's, so none of them isolates macOS 15.0 behaviour. See rule K3 productEvidenceIsNotProof.",
      "affects": [
        "TASK-260715-1uxx3i",
        "TASK-260715-1o9wjz"
      ],
      "resolutionOwner": "TASK-260715-1uxx3i",
      "matrixEffect": "One conditional row on macos.provider and one unarmed register entry, X1-C1. No authored entitlement, no allowlist change, no portal record, and no change to the Ceremony C1 authorization scope in either direction."
    }
  ],
