  "appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule": {
    "id": "G4",
    "statement": "com.apple.security.application-groups is granted only to a target whose OWN currently selected design uses one of the grants the entitlement actually confers on that platform. A possible future transport, a spec sentence, the shape of a target it resembles, and the cost of a second portal sitting are none of them a function. On iOS the two production targets qualify. The iOS probe pair does not: its own contract names no shared-container function, and G4 tests the target's own design, never an inherited one. On macOS no target qualifies today, so the key is prohibited on every macOS target, production and probe alike.",
    "surveyBasis": "The entitlement's macOS effect is not a matter of opinion: it is two sandbox-array-entitlement blocks in the system App Sandbox profile. Both were read in full at /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71) rather than inferred from documentation. They are the only two occurrences of the key in that profile.",
    "survey": [
      {
        "grant": "file-read-data on (application-group-literal suite \"\") and read-write-and-issue-extensions plus file-ioctl/mknod/revoke/search/mount/unmount on (application-group-regex suite \"/\")",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:305-315",
        "namespace": "home-relative",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": false,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "The group container itself. The host resolves it under the logged-in user's home, the root provider under /private/var/root, so it is not a shared container on macOS. Directly corroborated: two shipping packet-tunnel system extensions on this Mac ship temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write entries for /private/var/root/Library/Group Containers/, which is what you author when you need to cross that boundary."
      },
      {
        "grant": "network-bind and network-outbound on (application-group-regex suite \"/\")",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:307-314",
        "namespace": "home-relative",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": false,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "UNIX-domain sockets inside the group container. Same home-relative defect. It is also redundant for private use: application.sb:754-756 already allows network-bind and network-outbound on the target's OWN container with no entitlement at all."
      },
      {
        "grant": "file-read* and process-exec on /Library/Application Support/AppStore/GroupContent/<suite>",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:316-321",
        "namespace": "system-wide",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": true,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "App Store-delivered group content. This project ships development and Developer ID on macOS and never the Mac App Store (.spec/platform-distribution.md), so the path is never populated."
      },
      {
        "grant": "mach-lookup and mach-register on (global-name-prefix (string-append suite \".\"))",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:326-328",
        "namespace": "system-wide",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": true,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "The XPC channel, and the only grant that ever made the macOS App Group look load-bearing. TASK-260728-7ii1xz evaluated a custom XPC Mach service as candidate C and REJECTED it: it requires an exception entitlement that rule X1 puts under review, and it is dominated by the selected candidate D. With no XPC service there is no prefix to authorize."
      },
      {
        "grant": "ipc-posix* on (ipc-posix-name-prefix (string-append suite \"/\"))",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:329",
        "namespace": "system-wide",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": true,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "POSIX shared memory and semaphores in a group-prefixed namespace. This one does cross the root/user boundary and is easy to miss. No candidate transport proposed it, the accepted M1 lifecycle contract does not use it, and it is named here so that a future design cannot claim the entitlement was withheld without the option being seen."
      },
      {
        "grant": "read-only-and-issue-extensions and process-exec on ~/Library/Application Scripts/<suite>",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:459-465",
        "namespace": "home-relative",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": false,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "Scripting support. Home-relative, and this project ships no user scripts."
      }
    ],
    "privateContainerFinding": "TASK-260728-7ii1xz amendment M6 left the rows granted on the grounds that 'a private root-context container may still have uses this task did not survey'. Surveyed here, and the answer is no. (appsandbox-container-common) and (appsandbox-container-macos) at application.sb:107-108 are UNCONDITIONAL - they are not inside any (when (entitlement ...)) form - so a sandboxed target already has its own private container, derived from the application_container_id sandbox parameter, without any App Group entitlement. The only thing a group container adds over the container the provider already has is SHARING, and sharing is precisely what the root/user split breaks. A private group container is therefore strictly redundant, not merely unused.",
    "selectedTransport": "TASK-260728-7ii1xz selected candidate D: the provider owns a file-based system-domain keychain item, seeded once from the containing app over NETunnelProviderSession.sendProviderMessage, with the non-secret profile snapshot carried in providerConfiguration. Keychain, NE app message, providerConfiguration - none of the three is an App Group mechanism.",
    "iOSUnaffected": "On iOS the host and the appex run as the same user in the same context, so the group container and UserDefaults suite are genuinely shared and the .spec/architecture.md:111 design holds unchanged for PRODUCTION. ios.host and ios.provider keep the entitlement as required.",
    "reopensOnly": "This row reopens if, and only if, one of three things becomes true, each of which is a grant in the survey above rather than a general appeal to App Groups: (a) the macOS host-to-provider channel becomes a group-prefixed Mach service, i.e. the XPC candidate is revived; (b) it becomes a group-prefixed POSIX IPC object; or (c) the provider is moved out of root into a user context, which would make the group container genuinely shared. It does NOT reopen because a spec sentence mentions App Groups, because a shipping third-party product carries the key, or to spare a portal sitting. Reopening is an amendment under section 11, naming which of (a), (b) or (c) became true.",
    "ceremonyCostIsZero": "Withholding introduces no new human ceremony, which removes the operational argument verdict 03 rejected. The four iOS App IDs are already in c1AuthorizationScope.explicitlyNotAuthorized under ADR-024, so iOS provisioning already requires a later portal sitting that C1 does not cover. The App Group record is allocated at that sitting, alongside the iOS App IDs that are its only consumers. Nothing that C1 could have granted today is needed today. (r6: this sentence counted r4's two records until rule S1's count scan caught it - the third instance of the stale-summary class reviewer verdict 05 F1 named, and the one neither the reviewer nor r5 had spotted.)",
    "source": "/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71), read in full at lines 302-330 and 459-465. TASK-260728-7ii1xz_macos-credential-transport-decision.md sections 4, 5 and 6. Apple, Configuring app groups: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/configuring-app-groups",
    "probeRule": "r5, reviewer verdict 04 F3. A probe inherits identifiers and profile classes from the production shape it rehearses, but it does NOT inherit entitlements. Each probe row is tested against the probe task's own scope and acceptance criteria. TASK-260715-1jckn0 (iOS) and TASK-260715-1r0fxv (macOS) both scope their probe to the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle plus a versioned app message, and neither names a shared container, so no probe target on either platform is granted an App Group. If a probe is meant to prove that a capability can be provisioned at all, that is a VERIFICATION deliverable and must be written into the probe task as an acceptance criterion first; the entitlement then follows the criterion, not the other way round."
  },
  "keychainScopeRule": {
    "id": "K1",
    "statement": "On macOS the file-based (system-domain) keychain is controlled by per-item SecAccess ACLs. keychain-access-groups applies only to the Data Protection Keychain, which is unavailable outside a user context. A root-context provider targeting the system-domain keychain therefore never needs this entitlement, and the presence of the key in a shipping third-party provider is not evidence that it functions.",
    "replaces": "The amendmentRule formerly carried on targets[macos.provider].entitlements[keychain-access-groups]. That rule said the row would be amended to required if the resolved transport gave the provider direct keychain access. Its antecedent is now TRUE - candidate D does give the provider direct keychain access - while its consequent is FALSE, because the file-based keychain has no access groups. Left in place it was a live instruction to re-grant the row on exactly the reasoning TASK-260728-7ii1xz refutes.",
    "reopeningCondition": "The macos.provider row reopens only if the provider is moved into a user context, not merely because it touches a keychain.",
    "shippingProductsAreNotEvidence": "PureVPN's packet-tunnel system extensions carry keychain-access-groups. Under this rule that observation may not be used to argue the row back open: a key present in a signed bundle is not proof that the mechanism it names is the one the process uses.",
    "source": "Apple TN3137 'On Mac keychain APIs and implementations'; TASK-260728-7ii1xz_macos-credential-transport-decision.md sections 4-5 and 7.",
    "hostScope": "K1 governs the macOS PROVIDER only. It says nothing about the macOS host, which does run in a user context and does use the Data Protection Keychain, and that silence is what let r4 keep a shared access group on macos.host with no second member to share with. Rule K2 closes it: sharing is the entitlement's only function, so a host with no co-member is served by its default access group. Reviewer verdict 04 F1.",
    "osFloorScope": "K1's 'never needs this entitlement' clause is unconditional and stays that way - the Data Protection Keychain has no meaning in a root context on any OS version. What K1 did NOT state is that its supporting clause, 'the sandbox already grants what it needs', was read on macOS 26.5 alone. Rule K3 owns that version scope: it names the macOS 15.0 floor, names the owner that closes it, and pre-authorizes the temporary FILE exception an absent floor grant would require. That exception is not this entitlement, so arming it never reopens this row. Reviewer verdict 08 F1."
  },
  "downstreamConsequences": [
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260728-q5kjta",
      "consequence": "Ceremony C1 shrinks, and its board text was corrected in r5 to match this scope (verdict 04 F2). The sitting authorizes exactly four macOS App IDs - works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel - the Network Extensions capability on those four, and one Mac Development profile each with this Mac registered. It creates NO App Group record, enables the App Groups capability on NO App ID, and performs NO Keychain access group mutation (there is no such portal record). All four iOS App IDs are explicitly excluded under ADR-024. Fewer portal mutations, same single sitting. r7, verdict 06 F1: the sitting's board scope named the SUPERSEDED revision 2026-07-28.r5 - the revision verdict 05 rejected - while enumerating r6's mutations correctly. The scope now names the current revision, and rule A1 plus the extended board gate make a stale label fail closed instead of surviving as prose nothing reads. The authorized mutation set is unchanged."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-3jloqy",
      "consequence": "r6, verdict 05 F2: this task's own board contract was corrected, because it is the task that spends the C1 authorization unattended. Its description, scope, AC and checklist now state exactly portalMutationTaskContract (rule P1) - four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions on all four INCLUDING both hosts, one Mac Development profile each, no App Group record, no App Groups capability, no Keychain portal mutation, no iOS App ID, no distribution profile. The removed AC2 clause 'the packet-tunnel entitlement appears only on provider identifiers' was a material defect: Apple requires the entitlement on the containing app as well as the embedded provider, and R7 has required it on all eight target rows since r2, so provider-only verification would have passed a portal state that cannot build. Portal verification must also assert two NEGATIVES. App Groups: none of the four macOS App IDs has the capability enabled and no Mac Development profile's Entitlements dict carries com.apple.security.application-groups (assertion A16). Keychain: no macOS bundle declares keychain-access-groups (assertion A17). A17 is asserted against the signed BUNDLE, not the profile - a Mac Development profile legitimately carries the team-wide keychain-access-groups = ['<TEAMID>.*'] wildcard, and that wildcard is not a finding."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-uyju7n",
      "consequence": "Generated macOS targets must declare NEITHER com.apple.security.application-groups NOR keychain-access-groups, and neither the App Groups nor the Keychain Sharing capability may be added to them in Xcode. G1's instruction about the Register App Groups build setting now applies to iOS targets only. A macOS .entitlements file carrying either key fails A16 or A17. The macos.host development allowlist is consequently five authored keys plus three injected, and seven keys on developer-id (section 9.1)."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-33oofa",
      "consequence": "r9: recorded because rule D1 exposed that this task was a DECLARED consumer the contract never explained — its obligation was stated inside TASK-260715-uyju7n's entry, which is the macOS task. It is the iOS half and it carries the opposite instruction: both generated iOS production targets MUST declare com.apple.security.application-groups with the registered iOS-style literal and keychain-access-groups with the shared group (rules G1, G4, K2 - iOS is where all three are real), and rule G1's Register App Groups build setting applies HERE and only here. The two iOS PROBE targets must declare neither (G4 probeRule, assertion A18). Nothing is provisioned at Ceremony C1: all four iOS App IDs are deferred under ADR-024, so this task authors entitlements against identifiers that have no portal record yet, and assertion A9b is the check that no iOS development profile is expected."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-1r0fxv",
      "consequence": "The macOS probe pair carries no App Group and no keychain group. Its entitlement inspection (its AC5) gains A16 and A17 as negative checks. The probe's app-message path is unaffected - sendProviderMessage needs no App Group and no keychain group."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-3f4lxy",
      "consequence": "The macOS profile snapshot loader cannot read an App Group container and cannot fall back to one. On macOS the non-secret snapshot arrives in providerConfiguration; on iOS the App Group container remains correct. This is the same revision TASK-260728-7ii1xz section 8 already assigns."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-1tzaed",
      "consequence": "PlatformVPNIdentity (Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/VPNManagerRepository.swift:17) declares appGroupIdentifier as a non-optional String for both platforms, and production(for:) still throws. When that binding is authored there is no macOS App Group value to supply. The shape must change - most plainly to an optional or a platform-keyed identity - and this matrix is the input that forces it. No product source was changed by this revision."
    },
    {
      "owner": "spec amendment, owner TASK-260715-35nc5m",
      "consequence": ".spec/architecture.md:111 ('Shared mutable state is limited to an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references') and .spec/threat-model.md DF-02 / M-02 state an iOS property as if it were cross-platform. On macOS neither half holds: the group container is not shared and the secret lives in the root-unlocked system-domain keychain rather than the user's password-protected Data Protection Keychain. TASK-260728-7ii1xz already flags security-claims.md and threat-model.md M-02; architecture.md:111 is added here. This matrix records the divergence; it does not edit the spec."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-1jckn0",
      "consequence": "r5, verdict 04 F3: the iOS probe pair carries NO App Group and NO keychain group. Its host-to-provider exchange is NETunnelProviderSession.sendProviderMessage only, which is exactly what its AC3 already specifies, so no acceptance criterion changes and no scope is added. Its AC5 archive inspection gains assertion A18 as a negative check: neither probe bundle may declare com.apple.security.application-groups. If this task ever gains a real shared-container deliverable, that is the amendment path in the probe rows' reopensOnly - the acceptance criterion is written first and the entitlement follows it."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-379cpk",
      "consequence": "r5, verdict 04 F1: the macOS Keychain vault must NOT pass kSecAttrAccessGroup. On macOS the host's items live in its default access group, 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac, which the injected com.apple.application-identifier provides; naming the shared group would return errSecMissingEntitlement because macos.host no longer declares keychain-access-groups. On iOS the vault continues to name 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared explicitly, because there the group is real and shared with the appex. This is a platform branch in the vault, not a shared constant. Note that the macOS default group MOVED in r5 (rule K2 defaultAccessGroupLaw): under r4 an unqualified macOS write would have landed in the shared group, under r5 it lands in the app-ID group. No item has been written yet, so there is nothing to migrate - but the vault must not be authored against the r4 default."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-1o9wjz",
      "consequence": "r5: unchanged in substance and now unambiguous. The macOS extension credential resolver reads the provider-owned file-based system-domain keychain item seeded over the app-message channel (TASK-260728-7ii1xz candidate D); it never reads a Data Protection Keychain access group, and after r5 there is no macOS access group for it to read. On iOS the resolver reads 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared as before."
    },
    {
      "owner": "TASK-260715-1uxx3i",
      "consequence": "New in r10, from reviewer verdict 08 F1. The minimum-macOS lane gains one credential-free static check: read /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on the macos-15 runner and record whether a process holding com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension is granted file-read* and file-write* on the /Library/Keychains subpath, together with the com.apple.securityd.xpc and com.apple.SecurityServer Mach lookups. Absent means arm register entry X1-C1 under rule K3; present means retire it. This is a read of the runner's own system files - no credential, no signing, no portal access, and no new board element. TASK-260715-1o9wjz consumes the same answer at runtime, because an absent grant surfaces there as a provider-context failure rather than as a build finding."
    }
  ],
  "keychainLeastPrivilegeRule": {
    "id": "K2",
    "statement": "keychain-access-groups is granted only to a target that must share a Data Protection Keychain item with a NAMED second target in this contract. A target that only reads and writes its own items is already served by the default access group the signing toolchain gives it, so the entitlement would add signed authority with no consumer. On iOS the host and the appex are two such members and both are granted. On macOS there is no second member today, so the key is prohibited on all four macOS targets.",
    "defaultAccessGroupLaw": "The system forms each app's access-group array as the keychain-access-groups entitlement array first, then the application identifier, then the app groups; the FIRST element is the default group that keychain services uses when a caller names none. Two consequences this contract depends on. (a) A target with no keychain-access-groups entitlement still has a private default group equal to <TeamID>.<bundle id>, so withholding never costs a target access to its own items. (b) Withholding MOVES the default: macos.host's default group changes from 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared under r4 to 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac under r5. A consumer must not paper that over with an explicit kSecAttrAccessGroup naming the shared group - on macOS the correct call names no access group at all, and naming the shared one returns errSecMissingEntitlement.",
    "sharingIsTheOnlyFunction": "The entitlement confers exactly one thing beyond the default group: membership of a group that another target can also join. It does not unlock the Data Protection Keychain, does not change item durability, and does not survive anything the default group does not. Any argument for granting it that does not name a second reading target is therefore an argument for something the entitlement does not do.",
    "relationToK1": "K1 explains why the macOS PROVIDER can never be a member: the file-based system-domain keychain it uses has no access groups. K2 is the rule K1 did not cover - why the macOS HOST, which does use the Data Protection Keychain, still gets no custom group. Reviewer verdict 04 F1 named exactly that gap: the provider-side test was applied and the host-side one was not.",
    "reopeningCondition": "Per-row, in targets[].entitlements['keychain-access-groups'].reopensOnly. A row reopens only on a named second member, never on convenience or cross-platform symmetry.",
    "source": "Apple, Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/sharing-access-to-keychain-items-among-a-collection-of-apps); Apple TN3137, On Mac keychain APIs and implementations (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/technotes/tn3137-on-mac-keychains). Both fetched 2026-07-28.",
    "settledByDecisionNotOmission": "Every withheld keychain row states its own reopening condition, on both platforms and in both families. r1-r4 left the four probe rows prohibited with no such condition, which made them settled by omission - indistinguishable from an oversight, and reopenable by anyone who read the silence as 'nobody considered it'. Rule R31 now rejects a withheld row that states no reopening condition, which is the same discipline rule G4 already applies to App Groups."
  },
  "consumerDependencyContract": {
    "id": "D1",
    "statement": "Every board element this contract names as an obligation owner or an affected task is a DECLARED CONSUMER. A declared consumer must (a) appear in `consumers` and (b) transitively depend on TASK-260715-ypo7yo on the live board. Rules A1 and P1 pin the two consumers that carry a revision label, because a label can go stale. Every other consumer carries no label, so the only thing standing between it and being authored against an unaccepted matrix is the dependency edge — and a missing edge fails silently, because nothing reads a prose consumer list.",
    "history": "Found by this task in r9 during a self-audit after the r8 handoff; no reviewer verdict reported it. r1 wrote the `consumers` list as prose and r4–r8 grew it while the board DAG was never compared against it. Three declared consumers had no dependency path to this task at all, and four elements carrying obligations were absent from the list. This is the verdict-05 F2 class one field over: verdict 05 checked what a consumer SAYS, rules A1 and P1 check which revision it names, and nothing checked WHEN it runs.",
    "declaredConsumerSources": [
      "consumers[] — every element id in each entry",
      "openConstraints[].affects[] — each entry is an element id",
      "openConstraints[].resolutionOwner — an element id",
      "downstreamConsequences[].owner — every element id in the owner string; the spec-amendment owner is written as prose wrapped around an id",
      "any other string in this contract — see mentionCoverageRule"
    ],
    "mentionCoverageRule": "The derived set is NOT those named paths alone. Rule D1 scans EVERY string in this contract for an element id, because an obligation can be assigned in a sentence rather than in a list: TASK-260717-xempiv owned the Sparkle nested-code re-signing obligation for five revisions from inside two prose fields and appeared in no list, and TASK-260715-35nc5m owned the legacy migration decision from `legacy.migrationDecisionOwner` for eight. Every id found anywhere must be a declared consumer, a registered exemption, or this contract's own owner. There is no mention-only class: a contract that names a task names it for a reason.",
    "reachabilityRule": "TRANSITIVE, not direct. A declared consumer satisfies D1 if any chain of blockedBy edges from it reaches TASK-260715-ypo7yo. Requiring a direct edge from each of twenty consumers would flatten a DAG the board already orders correctly — thirteen of them reach this task through TASK-260715-3jloqy, which is the honest shape, because they consume the matrix through the portal state it produces.",
    "whyNotAStatusCheck": "A `done` consumer is not automatically exempt. Being finished is not evidence that it ran with this contract in hand — it is evidence that it ran, which is the failure mode this rule exists to catch. Only an UPSTREAM element is exempt, and the board gate verifies the upstream claim rather than accepting it.",
    "selfReference": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo",
    "exemptions": [
      {
        "element": "TASK-260728-7ii1xz",
        "basis": "upstream",
        "reason": "It BLOCKS this task: the macOS credential transport decision is an input to rules K1, K2 and G4, and its r2 amendment packet M1–M7 was applied here in r4. Requiring it to depend on this task would be a cycle. It is also `done`, but that is not the basis — the basis is the direction of the edge.",
        "boardFact": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo.blockedBy contains TASK-260728-7ii1xz."
      }
    ],
    "exemptionBasisAllowed": [
      "upstream"
    ],
    "boardChange": {
      "revision": "2026-07-28.r9",
      "edge": "TASK-260715-29ws8l blockedBy TASK-260715-ypo7yo",
      "closes": [
        "TASK-260715-29ws8l",
        "TASK-260715-1o9wjz",
        "TASK-260715-3f4lxy"
      ],
      "why": "One edge, three gaps. TASK-260715-29ws8l already blocks TASK-260715-1o9wjz and TASK-260715-3f4lxy, so ordering it after this contract orders all three. The edge is semantically real, not a scheduling trick: 29ws8l AC2 requires it to specify what travels in 'App Group and providerConfiguration data' and AC4 requires it to specify 'Keychain accessibility, access group' — on macOS rule G4 prohibits the App Group on every target and rule K2 prohibits the keychain access group on every target, so authoring 29ws8l without this matrix reproduces exactly the iOS-only assumption OC-1 was raised against.",
      "cycleCheck": "The whole blockedBy graph was checked for cycles with this edge added before it was written: none. TASK-260715-ypo7yo depends only on TASK-260728-7ii1xz, which depends on nothing.",
      "footprint": "One dependency edge added. No board element created, deleted, re-parented, or re-scoped by this rule."
    },
    "staleClaimsFound": [
      "REACHABILITY — TASK-260715-1o9wjz: declared in `consumers` since r1 and assigned a downstream consequence in r5. Its two blockers were the credential-transport decision and TASK-260715-29ws8l, neither of which reached this task, so it had no path here.",
      "REACHABILITY — TASK-260715-3f4lxy: declared in `consumers`, assigned a downstream consequence, and named in OC-4.affects. Its three blockers were the credential-transport decision, TASK-260715-29ws8l, and one unrelated task, so it had no path here.",
      "REACHABILITY — TASK-260715-29ws8l: named in OC-1.affects. Its two blockers were the credential-transport decision and one unrelated task, so it had no path here — and it blocks the other two, which is why one edge closes all three.",
      "LIST — TASK-260715-29ws8l: named in OC-1.affects, absent from `consumers`.",
      "LIST — TASK-260715-2hhh7x: named in OC-1.affects, absent from `consumers` (reachable, so the list was the only defect).",
      "LIST — TASK-260715-35nc5m: `legacy.migrationDecisionOwner` and a downstreamConsequences owner, absent from `consumers` (reachable).",
      "LIST — TASK-260717-xempiv: assigned the Sparkle nested-code re-signing obligation in two prose fields, absent from `consumers` (reachable)."
    ],
    "boardGate": {
      "graphQueries": [
        "list(type=task) { id blockedBy }",
        "list(type=bug) { id blockedBy }"
      ],
      "graphNote": "Both element types are read. A bug can carry a dependency edge, so reading tasks alone would compute a closure over a subgraph and could call a consumer unreachable — or reachable — on incomplete data.",
      "requires": [
        "every declared consumer resolves to a live board element",
        "every declared consumer that is not a registered exemption transitively depends on TASK-260715-ypo7yo",
        "every registered exemption is genuinely UPSTREAM: this task transitively depends on it",
        "the derived mention set equals the declared consumer set plus the exemptions plus this contract's own owner, in both directions"
      ]
    },
    "gate": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_check-portal-consumer.py adds a third block, rule D1, which reads the live dependency graph and computes the transitive closure. validate_matrix.py cannot read the board; R37 checks only that this pin is well formed, that the mention set and the consumer list agree, and that every exemption carries an allowed basis and a reason.",
    "source": "Self-audit, r9. The consumer list in this contract, openConstraints, downstreamConsequences, and the live board dependency graph. ADR-027 (deferral is `blocked`, never an unlinked backlog item) is the same principle applied to deferral rather than to consumption.",
    "whyBlockersAreNotNamed": "The two unrelated blockers that stood in these chains are described rather than named. Naming them would make them mentions, and rule D1's own mentionCoverageRule would then require them to be declared consumers of a contract they do not consume — a rule that manufactures its own violations by describing them. The board gate reads the real edges, so nothing is lost by not spelling the ids here."
  },
  "numericClaimContract": {
    "id": "N1",
    "statement": "A count, or an enumerated key list, stated in prose about an entitlement allowlist is a PROJECTION of the target rows plus the signing-generated table. Each one is registered here and recomputed by the gate in both artifacts. A number written out by hand is a claim nothing checks.",
    "history": "Found by this task in r9. §9.1 records the class biting once already: r3's prose said the macos.host development allowlist was 'exactly seven keys' over a list of ten, and r4 corrected it by hand to nine. r5 then moved it again to eight, when rule K2 withdrew keychain-access-groups from every macOS target. Three revisions, three different numbers, corrected by hand each time. r6 built a count scan for App Group RECORD counts (rule S1's recordCountClaimRule) after verdict 05 exposed a stale one, and stopped there — so the other live count class stayed unchecked in both artifacts for three more revisions.",
    "derivation": "allowlist(target, channel) = { entitlement keys whose status is in entitlementClassification.authoredStatuses } ∪ signingGenerated[platform].always ∪ (channel == development ? signingGenerated[platform].developmentChannelOnly : ∅). This is the same expression §9.1 renders and the same one assertions A10a–A10d apply; N1 makes the prose numbers agree with it instead of restating it.",
